---
title: Search
summary: One search brain, two ways to run it. Ship a static index to an on-device worker for zero-JS-cost queries, or query it on the server. A no-JS fallback page comes for free.
related:
  - pharos/remotes
  - pharos/emit
  - pharos/shell
---

Pharos builds a search index from your collections' section documents — the prose split by heading, so a hit lands on the exact section. You choose where the query runs: on the server, or on the visitor's device against a static index. **The search on this site is the second kind** — a worker over a prerendered file, so a query costs no server round-trip.

> **When to use.** Any docs site benefits from search. Prefer the static-index path for a prerendered site — it is free to serve and works offline. Reach for the server query when the corpus is private, huge, or changes per request.

## The static path (recommended)

Emit the index once, at build, and let an on-device worker query it:

```ts
// src/routes/docs/search.json/+server.ts — the prerendered index
import { site } from '$lib/site.server';
export const prerender = true;
export const GET = site.emit.search();
```

```svelte
<!-- the ⌘K palette, over that index -->
<script>
  import { Search } from 'ogygia/pharos';
</script>

<Search base="/docs" placeholder="Search the docs" />
```

`<Search>` is an [island](/docs/regions/client-islands): the server renders just the trigger (a real link to the fallback page), and on first intent — ⌘K, `/`, or a click — it loads the palette and spins up an [Orama](https://orama.com) worker that indexes the static file **off the main thread**. Ranking marks matched terms and boosts title matches. No server touches a query.

## The server path

When the index shouldn't ship — a private or per-request corpus — query the brain on the server via a [remote](/docs/pharos/remotes):

```ts
export const { search } = remotes(site, { base: '/docs' });
```

```svelte
<script>
  import { search } from '$lib/site.remote';
  let q = $state('');
</script>

<input bind:value={q} />
{#each await search(q) as hit}
  <a href={hit.href}>{hit.title} — {hit.excerpt}</a>
{/each}
```

Same brain (`site.search`), same hits; only the location differs.

## The no-JS fallback

Search must work without JavaScript. The `<Search>` trigger is a real `<a href="/docs/search">` — with JS it opens the palette, without JS it follows the link to a plain results page you render from a server load:

```ts
// src/routes/docs/search/+page.server.ts
import { site } from '$lib/site.server';
export const load = async ({ url }) => ({
  q: url.searchParams.get('q') ?? '',
  hits: await site.search(url.searchParams.get('q') ?? '', { base: '/docs' })
});
```

```svelte
<script>
  import { SearchPage } from 'ogygia/pharos';
  let { data } = $props();
</script>

<SearchPage q={data.q} hits={data.hits} />
```

Same index, same ranking, zero client JS — the graceful floor under the palette.

## Scoping

`site.search(q, { in: [collection] })` narrows the query to one collection — useful when a site weaves several (docs + blog) and a box should search just one. The static index carries every collection; the worker filters by scope.

## Do / don't

- **Do** ship the static index for a prerendered site. An on-device worker is faster to serve and works offline; the server never sees a keystroke.
- **Do** keep the fallback page. It is one server load, and it means search survives with JS disabled or still loading.
- **Don't** mint a `search` remote *and* ship the index for the same box — pick one path. The index is for the worker; the remote is for server queries.
- **Don't** build your own index off the raw bodies. `site.emit.search()` splits by section so a hit deep-links to the right heading.
